Slide 1 — SSL Certificate Automation Spike

“The purpose of this spike was to determine whether our SSL renewal process can be automated, identify which tools and APIs are involved, understand what we can reuse from our existing TeamCity process, and define what implementation would look like.”

You can then say:

“The short answer is yes—the process is technically feasible to automate.”

Slide 2 — Executive Summary

“The main conclusion from the spike is that we shouldn't build one large script that tries to do everything. We can use TeamCity as the orchestrator and separate the workflow into different API-driven steps.”

Then explain the boxes:

“DigiCert handles certificate renewal and retrieval. Fastly handles deployment for our external certificates, and TeamCity coordinates the overall process using PowerShell and parameters.”

Slide 3 — Scope Boundary

“This slide clarifies ownership. DevOps automation will handle renewing the existing certificate through DigiCert, retrieving it, deploying it to Fastly, and validating the result.”

Then:

“Domain validation and pre-validation remain with SysAdmin, based on what we agreed during the spike. Billing and payment setup also remain outside the automation.”

This is important because it prevents implementation scope from growing later.

Slide 4 — Current-State Findings

This is one of your more important slides.

“One of the biggest findings was that we don't need to start completely from scratch. We already have a TeamCity job called NSX-ALB Certificate Issue that uses a parameter-driven PowerShell workflow to create a CSR, issue a certificate and upload it.”

Then explain the limitation:

“However, that process uses our internal SILAC SubCA certificate request flow. It doesn't currently call DigiCert for external certificate renewal, and it doesn't deploy certificates to Fastly.” 
For the Virtual Service line:

“I also didn't find logic in the existing script that updates the certificate binding on the NSX-ALB Virtual Service. So that's something we'd need to confirm if we extend this pattern to internal certificates.”


Slide 5 — Tool Capability Assessment

“I looked at each tool individually to determine what role it would have in the automation.”

Then very simply:

“DigiCert provides the APIs needed for certificate renewal, status tracking and retrieval. Fastly provides APIs for managing the certificates and private keys used by our external domains. TeamCity gives us the orchestration layer—parameters, scripts, permissions, artifacts and execution history.”

Then:

“So my recommendation is to reuse the existing TeamCity pattern, but build the new DigiCert and Fastly integrations around it.”

Slide 6 — Recommended Target Workflow

For this one, literally walk left to right:

“The workflow would start either on a schedule or based on an expiration alert. TeamCity calls DigiCert to renew the certificate, checks the order until issuance completes, and retrieves the certificate and chain.”

Then:

“The renewed certificate is deployed to Fastly, and finally we validate the public HTTPS endpoint to make sure the new certificate is actually being served.”

The validation piece is particularly important:

“I don't want the pipeline to consider an API upload successful as the end of the process. Success means the public endpoint is actually presenting the renewed certificate.”

That's a very good point to emphasize to your manager.

Slide 7 — Implementation Plan

“For implementation, I'm recommending that we deliver this incrementally rather than trying to automate everything at once.”

Then summarize:

“First we confirm the required IDs, permissions and service accounts. Then build the DigiCert renewal module, followed by Fastly deployment. After that we add validation and reporting. Finally, we pilot it against one lower-risk service before expanding to the remaining certificates.”

You don't need to explain all five phases individually unless they ask.

Slide 8 — Implementation Work Breakdown

This slide is essentially answering: “What tickets do we create after the spike?”

Explain it this way:

“This converts the spike findings into actual implementation stories. Rather than having one large implementation ticket, I'm recommending five smaller deliverables.”

Then briefly explain:

“Story one builds the DigiCert renewal and retrieval script. Story two handles Fastly deployment. Story three puts those pieces together in TeamCity. Story four adds end-to-end certificate validation and reporting. Story five covers the production pilot, rollback procedure and operational documentation.”

The key point:

“That way each component can be developed and tested independently before we enable the full production workflow.”

What does “Pilot + rollback runbook” mean?

Pilot = test the completed automation against one selected lower-risk certificate/domain first, rather than immediately enabling it for every SILAC domain.

Rollback runbook = documented instructions for restoring the previous certificate/configuration if the automated deployment causes a problem.

So if your manager asks:

“Before rolling this across all certificates, we'd test one service and have a documented recovery procedure in case validation fails.”




Slide 9 — Open Questions and Technical Debt

Don't present these as problems with the spike. Present them as implementation decisions that still need to be finalized.

You can say:

“The spike confirms feasibility, but there are several implementation details we should resolve before production rollout.”

A few of these you've actually already answered through your latest Fastly investigation.

For example, Fastly TLS model is no longer really an open question. Your Fastly screenshots confirmed that the existing certificates are DigiCert-issued certificates being managed in Fastly. I would update that item before the presentation.

Then explain the remaining major questions:

“We still need to confirm where the private key will originate and how it will be securely handled, map the DigiCert certificate or order IDs to each domain, determine where API credentials will live, define the exact validation criteria, and establish the rollback behavior.”

And then technical debt:

“The technical-debt section is really about what we don't want to introduce while implementing this—hard-coded API tokens, no dry-run capability, no audit trail, or going to production without a rollback path.”

That's probably all you need to say.

Slide 10 — Manager Decisions / Approval

Your manager really needs to leave the meeting answering three things:

“The main decisions I'm looking for from this review are whether we're aligned to move from the spike into implementation, who should confirm the remaining billing and approval dependencies, and which Fastly service we want to use as the initial pilot.”
